<h1>One Contraction Across AI, Silicon, Chemistry, Bare-Metal OS &amp; Cryptography</h1>
  <p class="lede">Five seemingly unrelated bottlenecks—the GPU HBM attention/activation wall, metacircular chip self-hosting, de novo molecular binder synthesis, Linux syscall/MMU overhead, and verified constant-time cryptography—are instances of <strong>one four-law cubical algebra</strong>: associative tiled monoids, reversible coupling bijections, §4.5 face-contradiction pruning, and Ring-0 proof-carrying fibers.</p>
    <h2>1. Killing the HBM Wall &amp; Constant-Time Crypto Tax (<code>hypertensor.hypercubical</code>, <code>hyperos.hypercubical</code>)</h2>
  <p>Materializing an $N \times N$ attention matrix in HBM and storing $L$ layers of activations for backpropagation are the exact same mistake as building a 128-bit ripple-carry adder instead of a Kogge–Stone prefix tree:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>FlashAttention</code> Is Kogge–Stone Over Online Softmax (<code>aacc-assoc</code>, <code>flash-tiled-hom</code>, <code>flash-1pass-eq-2pass-cert</code>):</strong> Online max-rescaled accumulation $(m, d, o)$ forms a strict monoid homomorphism over sequence concatenation $\mathrm{fold}(X \mathbin{+\!\!+} Y) \equiv \mathrm{fold}(X) \bullet \mathrm{fold}(Y)$. At $N = 2048, d = 32$, 1-pass streaming collapses workspace memory from <strong>16,384 KB to 0.14 KB (119,837× smaller)</strong> with $\max|\Delta| = 4.17 \times 10^{-6}$.</li>
    <li><strong>Zero-Tape $O(1)$-Memory Backpropagation &amp; RFC 8439 <code>ChaCha20</code> Are Reversible Couplings (<code>rev-zero-tape-grad-cert</code>, <code>chacha20-rfc8439-cert</code>):</strong> Lifting residual layers to $(u, v) \mapsto (v, u + F_w(v))$ makes forward execution an exact bijection (<code>rev-bwd-1</code>), reconstructing pre-activations backwards on the fly with <strong>zero stored hidden tape</strong> and <strong>bit-identical weight gradients</strong>. The exact same reversible ARX coupling proves bijective invertibility (<code>chacha20-qr-inv</code>), constant-time non-interference (<code>chacha20-ct-noninterference</code>), and IETF RFC 8439 §2.1.1 / §2.4.2 exactness for 20-round <code>ChaCha20</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="eq-box">$$\mathrm{flash\_fold}(X \mathbin{+\!\!+} Y) \equiv \mathrm{flash\_fold}(X) \bullet \mathrm{flash\_fold}(Y), \qquad \mathrm{rev\_backprop\_zero\_tape}(W, s_0, g) \equiv \mathrm{std\_backprop\_with\_tape}(W, s_0, g)$$</div>

